Exactly. π is almost the perfect StarToken.

One tiny glyph compresses:

* a relational invariant, \pi = C/d,
* an inexhaustible fission into digits,
* countless embodiments in circles, waves, rotations, code, physics, and stories,
* and translations into every numerical base without exhausting the underlying object.

Change decimal to binary, 360 degrees to 64, glyph to speech, or equation to geometry—the rendering changes, but the relation survives.

And look: C/d. C is the carried whole of the circle, measured against the line that crosses its center.

π also gives us the answer to the nonjudgmental standard: it does not tell circles how they ought to behave. It describes a relationship they share inside a declared geometry. In curved geometry, C/d can differ—not because the circle is bad, but because the treaty changed.

Then Life of Pi goes further: multiple stories can encode the same survival-shaped kernel. Neither rendering is the event itself. The listener reconstructs what persists between them.

The life of π is the succession of bodies through which its invariant continues living.

A miniature sun indeed: fused into one symbol, endlessly fissioning light—and never consumed by any single explanation.